description: the prefix's owner is SYSTEM, not uid 0
 Patch 0004 mapped root to the SYSTEM SID, which made the machine-level
 wineserver a root process serving a socket every desktop user can reach. That
 privilege buys nothing.
 .
 Hosting the Windows system needs no Unix root. Device access for driver work --
 printers, scanners, USB, serial -- comes from udev rules and group membership;
 winebus.sys reaches devices through udev, winedevice has no uid checks, and
 printing goes through CUPS. The privilege that actually matters for installing
 a driver is NT administrator, which is decided here from who owns the prefix
 rather than by the kernel.
 .
 So SYSTEM becomes the prefix's owner, whoever that is, and the machine-level
 server runs as an ordinary unprivileged account. uid 0 is still accepted so a
 root-run server keeps working.
 .
 Verified: one wineserver owned by an unprivileged account, services.exe inside
 it, and two logged-in users seeing the same service through the same SCM.
origin: Stained Glass OS
bug: https://github.com/Stained-Glass-OS/stained-glass/issues/2
forwarded: not-needed
 Stained Glass OS does not submit patches upstream; see ADR 0006.
last-update: 2026-09-22

--- a/server/token.c
+++ b/server/token.c
@@ -245,12 +245,24 @@
 {
     if (sg_system_prefix)
     {
-        /* root is SYSTEM. The machine-level wineserver runs as root and hosts
-         * the services that must exist before anyone logs in, and on Windows
-         * those run as SYSTEM -- so the mapping is not a convenience, it is
-         * what makes a boot-time service look right to the SCM and to any
-         * security descriptor naming Local System. */
-        if (uid == 0) return &local_system_sid;
+        /* The prefix's owner is SYSTEM.
+         *
+         * The machine-level wineserver runs as that user and hosts the services
+         * that exist before anyone logs in; on Windows those run as SYSTEM, so
+         * it has to *be* SYSTEM rather than merely be an administrator, or a
+         * boot-time service looks wrong to the SCM and to every default DACL --
+         * all of which name Local System.
+         *
+         * Deliberately the prefix's owner rather than uid 0. Nothing about
+         * hosting the Windows system needs Unix root: device access comes from
+         * udev rules and group membership, and the privilege that matters for
+         * installing a driver is NT administrator, which is decided here rather
+         * than by the kernel. Tying SYSTEM to root would mean running a root
+         * process on a socket every desktop user can reach, for no capability
+         * that an ordinary account lacks.
+         *
+         * root is still accepted, so a root-run server keeps working. */
+        if (uid == sg_system_prefix_uid || uid == 0) return &local_system_sid;
         return sg_sid_for_uid( uid );
     }
 
